<EnrolledLayout title="Family Handbook | Wild Pear Preschool & Summer Camp">
	<LegalPage2
		title="Family Handbook"
		description="A full transcription of the 2025-2026 Family Handbook, covering our policies and procedures from Mission through Mandated Reporting."
		pubDate="2025-2026"
		pubDateLabel="School Year"
		bodyHeadings
	>
		<div class="not-prose mb-10 flex justify-center">
			<img
				src="/images/wp-logo-white.png"
				alt="Wild Pear Preschool logo"
				width="1740"
				height="740"
				class="h-auto w-64"
				loading="eager"
			/>
		</div>

		<blockquote>
			<p>Welcome families,</p>
			<p>
				It is a joy to welcome you into our program! Please take some time to read through this
				handbook so you will understand our expectations for our time together. You can always
				access the handbook from our website (under the "resources" tab) or in hard copy form in
				the policy notebook. The policy notebook also has more detailed information about certain
				topics, you are welcome to look through it and request copies of your own. As always, if
				you have any questions or concerns, please let us know. We would love to talk with you
				more about our policies and procedures and can schedule a meeting or phone call to open up
				a discussion.
			</p>
			<p>Warmly,<br />Jeannie and Susan</p>
		</blockquote>

		<h2>Mission</h2>
		<p>
			At Wild Pear, our goal is to meet children where they are at developmentally and encourage
			them to move forward in their own unique way. With an emphasis on social and emotional
			health and grounded in a foundation of connected relationships, we engage in activities that
			invite children to practice a wide range of skill building. Providing time away from a busy
			fast-paced life, we offer space and time for holistic multifaceted education inspired by
			direct experiences with food, gardens, and nature.
		</p>

		<h2>Inclusion and Anti-Bias Policy</h2>
		<p>
			Wild Pear Preschool is an inclusive and welcoming school, meaning your family will be
			accepted and valued here. We will see and honor you and your child's race, religion, gender,
			ability, orientation, class, country of origin, immigration status, language, and education
			level. We respect all family structures; whether you are connected by blood, or law, or love
			alone, you are welcome here. We believe that diversity in our community gives us an
			invaluable opportunity to widen our perspectives and enrich our experiences. We also
			recognize that discomfort/confusion sometimes accompany newness, and there may be a learning
			curve when it comes to various presentations of diversity. We are glad to help point you
			towards some wonderful resources to help support your personal learning. Equally, we invite
			you to share resources with us that can better educate us to the beautiful breadth of
			experiences in your family. In order to help maintain an atmosphere of respect and safety we
			always ask for communication that honors the identities of the people in our community.
		</p>
		<p>
			We actively teach anti-bias curriculum at WPP and like all aspects of your child's education
			you have a right and responsibility to know how topics are being presented. While we draw
			from a variety of resources, the work of Louise Derman Sparks, Katie Kissinger, Teaching
			Tolerance, and Rethinking Schools are a regular inspiration to us. As with all topics at
			school, anti-bias topics are presented and discussed in an age-appropriate and relevant
			manner.
		</p>
		<p>
			Anti-bias work, like most work with young children, is ongoing, ever growing, and requires
			intentional self-work and introspection on the part of the adults entrusted to teach them. We
			cannot say it's the easiest part of our work, but we feel it is perhaps one of the most
			worthwhile. We as individuals are still learning and will undoubtedly make blunders and
			missteps on our own journeys, but we are committed to keep walking forward towards justice and
			equity. If you ever feel that a part of your or your child's identity is being disrespected,
			ignored, or uncelebrated, please bring it to our attention and we will find a way to correct
			it.
		</p>
		<p>
			We don't need to know all the personal beliefs, values, and practices of the families in our
			program, but we do ask that parents support this tender aspect of the work we do at WPP. If
			you have questions or would like help to find resources to support your own learning, please
			do not hesitate to ask. If you find you are unable to walk in this direction with us, it's
			likely that Wild Pear Preschool is not a good fit for your family and we send you with love as
			you look for care elsewhere.
		</p>

		<h2>Hours, Calendar, and Closures</h2>
		<p>
			We are open Monday through Friday 7:30 am until 5:30 pm. Our school year begins in September
			and runs until mid-June. We will remain open and offer weekly summer camps for school age
			children (5-10 years old) during June, July and August. We try hard to offer a handful of
			summer camp slots to the currently enrolled younger siblings (2-4 years old) of school age
			children who will be attending camp. More information about this will be sent to eligible
			families in early Spring.
		</p>

		<h3>Extreme weather and emergency closures</h3>
		<p>
			If both PPS and NCSD are delayed, closing early, or closed all day due to extreme weather,
			WPP will do the same. If their decisions don't align, we will make our own decision and
			communicate it as soon as possible.
		</p>

		<AlertWarning title="Winter Weather, Ice and Snow">
			We love the magic of having the children at school on snowy days where we will gear up and
			play outside as long as it is safe to do so. If the roads are dangerously icey in the
			morning, we may have a delayed start and will send an email out early in the morning or
			perhaps even the night before. If there is a forecast of heavy snow or icy roads in the
			afternoon we may close early. We love your children but have no desire to be snowed in with
			them overnight and want to ensure you and our teachers have enough time to pick up and drive
			home safely. If there is a winter storm that presents a likely risk of power outages or
			falling trees we will be closed.
		</AlertWarning>

		<AlertWarning title="Summer Weather, Heat and Smoke">
			Summer days are glorious here and the combination of lush shade and a breeze off the creek
			creates a play space cool enough for even the hottest days. During wildfire season our air
			quality can be dramatically impacted. If the AQ is 150-200 (red) we will be open for a half
			day only (7:30-12:45), with pick up being directly after lunch. If the AQ is above 200 (purple
			or maroon) we will be closed. If the school is in a Level 1 evacuation status for wild fires
			we will remain open but will close immediately if we move to a Level 2 evacuation status. If
			the evacuation status jumps suddenly to a Level 3 while children are in care we will evacuate
			with them following our emergency plan detailed in a later heading.
		</AlertWarning>

		<p>
			<strong>Other unscheduled closures:</strong> We can operate in ratio as long as we have at
			least 2 staff members present for the preschool and 2 staff members for Sprouts, but if too
			many staff members urgently and unexpectedly need to be out at the same time we may need to
			close for some or all of the school day. If the school were ever to be without water,
			electricity, or heat we would need to close immediately. Any flooding, fire, gas leak or other
			emergency would also result in immediate closure.
		</p>
		<p>
			We understand and respect that for many families, preschool is more than an enrichment
			activity and it is often critical child care as well. In the rare case of an unscheduled
			closure, we will notify you as soon as possible and try our best to remain open if we can do
			so safely. However, <strong
				>it is your responsibility to arrange for back up care in the event that we do need to
				close unexpectedly.</strong
			>
		</p>

		<h2>Wild Pear Campus</h2>
		<p>
			Any time you are here you are welcome to park in our small lot, in the two side by side
			driveways, or on our side of the street. (The neighbor across the street can get a little
			fussy about people parking in front of his house and it's important to us to keep a good
			relationship with him.) You can also park in the church parking lot.
		</p>
		<p>We have 3 large fenced play yards on campus:</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				The "<strong>Lower Yard</strong>" which is to the right of the blue house (9411), and can be
				accessed from the gate at the small parking lot, or by walking down and around behind the
				blue house after entering from the gate on the right at the end of the driveways.
			</li>
			<li>
				The "<strong>Sprouts Yard</strong>" which is directly behind the white house (9433) and can
				be accessed through the left gate at the end of the driveways, and
			</li>
			<li>
				The "<strong>School Age Yard</strong>" which is behind the Sprouts Yard has an entrance that
				is near the teal garage which can be accessed by walking straight back after entering from
				the gate on the right at the end of the driveways, or by walking up and around after
				entering from the gate at the parking lot.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Please help us teach children that at school "gates are for grown ups" and make sure all
			gates are closed securely behind you when you exit a space. The gates do occasionally give us
			trouble, and if this is the case, please let a teacher or director know so we can attend to
			it.
		</p>
		<p>
			Please help us protect the plants and wildlife on the property by staying on the paths
			(graveled/bark chiped/paved spaces) while walking place to place outside these designated
			play yards. Because areas outside the play yards are less maintained, we often have insects
			and plants living there which may not be ideal for children to wander into. There are also 2
			sets of outside stairs between the upper and lower yard of the preschool that are not
			maintained, not licensed, and thus not available for families to use. Instead, please walk on
			the gravel path or the right of way in front of the house near the street. In order to secure
			the safety and well being of the children while on site, no children under age 5 are allowed
			to move around the campus without being directly accompanied by an adult. This includes going
			between sites, going inside to use the bathroom, or walking to their car. Also, please never
			leave a child under age 5 in your car, even if you're nearby and just need to snag a sibling
			real quick. You are welcome to let yourself in the gates and doors as needed anytime your
			child is scheduled to be there.
		</p>
		<p>
			We know it's tempting to stick around and play or chat with friends after pick up, however, to
			remain in accordance with our liability insurance, families need to leave campus after picking
			up their children and cannot stay and play in unoccupied play yards, in parking lots/driveways,
			or other parts of campus.
		</p>

		<h2>Family Expectations for Arrival and Departure</h2>
		<h3>Arrival</h3>
		<p>
			Most days, children will be dropped off outside in their play yard. If you show up and don't
			see anyone, double check the whiteboard or pop inside to see if there is an inside drop off or
			a teacher who is on their way out. You are welcome to let yourself in the gates and doors any
			time your child is scheduled to be in care! Before you leave, make sure your child is set for
			the weather: sunscreen, rain gear, layers, etc. We cannot send the preschool children up to the
			covered area alone to grab their jacket or use the bathroom without us, and once you leave it
			may be a while before a group can head up together. This is also why it's very helpful if you
			can have your child either use the bathroom right before you leave your house or bring them
			into the bathroom when you arrive at school.
		</p>
		<p>
			Please use the app to scan the QR code located on the policy note book on the "parent counter"
			in the covered area. If for whatever reason the app isn't working, or your child is dropped off
			by someone without access, let a teacher know so they can sign your child in on that day. The
			parent counter area will have a lot of information for you, including a whiteboard that will
			list reminders, low concern health alerts, and information about the learning happening at
			school. Before you head out, it is your responsibility to make sure a teacher knows you are
			passing off your child (this can be done simply with eye contact and a wave, or by
			conversation, greeting, fancy handshake, etc).
		</p>
		<p>
			If it's likely that your child will need the focused support of a teacher during drop off, we
			ask that you leave time for the goodbye transition to happen before 8:15. Once the school day
			starts teachers need to shift their attention to managing breakfast and circle time. All
			children need to be at school, ready to learn by 8:30. Morning circle is a time for us to
			recognize each child, to share the plan for the day, and to set the tone for the day's work and
			it's important that your child joins us. Children may not arrive after 8:30 unless a special
			arrangement has been made in advance, or in the case of an emergency. Please use the app to let
			us know if you're going to be absent.
		</p>
		<p>
			To us arriving at school, "ready to learn" means that the child is fully awake, has had time to
			freshen up and has a clean body and clean fresh clothes on. And a ready to learn child needs to
			have time to separate from their parent and join the group in a way that feels respectful and
			unhurried, if not always "happy". Children who are ready to learn are comfortable physically,
			emotionally, and socially, which allows their brains and bodies to shift into school mode and
			engage with the work at hand.
		</p>
		<p>
			If your child is having a difficult time transitioning into their school day, don't worry! We
			view this as typical behavior and you have many different options:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Stay for a few minutes and play with your child</li>
			<li>Invite your child to wave to you through/over the fence</li>
			<li>See if your child is interested in holding their family picture while you go</li>
			<li>Ask a teacher to hold your child's body or hand while you leave</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			We know leaving your child when they are upset can be tricky, especially if you're in a hurry.
			Rushing through drop off, leaving without saying goodbye, or showing your own discomfort can
			accidentally give your child the message that you're not sure if they will have a successful
			day at school. When it's time for you to walk out the gate, we find it's best to give your
			child a clear message that you are leaving, that you will be back, and that you are confident
			they will have a good day at school and then leave. Coming back for "just one more hug" or
			lingering after you have said you're leaving can be very confusing to children and also to
			teachers who may be trying to assess the need and timing for their support.
		</p>

		<h3>Departure</h3>
		<p>
			Pick up will most likely happen outside for all children. It is very important for us to make
			sure children are safe and connected to their grown ups at the end of the day. So similar to
			drop off, you will need to both sign your child out using the app and make sure a teacher knows
			you are leaving with your child. Children will only be released to custodial parents and/or
			legal guardians unless you specify otherwise on their enrollment forms. In order to give
			families more flexibility during emergencies or unexpected circumstances, we also allow parents
			to use the app to send the information of a last minute pick up person. Anyone picking up the
			child who is not recognized by the teacher may be asked to show their ID. Anytime you are
			having an "out of the norm" pick up person, it helps us so much if you send a message and give
			us a heads up. If your child is not picked up on time, you will be charged $1.00 for every
			minute past 5:30 that they remain in care. That time will be based on the teacher's clock. You
			will need to pay the fee before your child can attend school the following day.
		</p>
		<p>
			Children are often tired and ready to head home when they see their parents, other times they
			might like a few minutes to finish their work or want to share something with you. You are
			welcome to stay for a while and watch your child play or chat with other adults. Please take
			time when you first arrive to greet your child and make a plan with them about how long you'll
			stay, limiting your time to 10 minutes.
		</p>
		<p>
			We expect young children to be very curious about the consistency of rules and boundaries, it
			is important work for them to discover who has power and how flexible various limits might be.
			When you are here with your child, you are their number one grown up, and it is very likely
			that they will have some strong feelings about being separated from you during the day. At pick
			up, your child may be eager to reunite with you and lose focus of their prior work. If your
			child demonstrates behaviors that are increasingly loud, big, and fast, or they seem
			disengaged, distracted, or begin to clearly test limits and boundaries, we believe it is their
			way of saying "I'm uncomfortable because I'm unsure about who is in charge right now, I'm ready
			to shift." It's also not uncommon to see children escalate into conflict with their peers or
			have outbursts of big emotions signaling they are ready for you to take the lead and help them
			transition out of their school day. At this point, we encourage you to respect your child's
			message and cut your planned visit short, tell your child it's time to leave, and follow
			through promptly. We understand that this may mean that you need to leave without cleaning up
			or finishing a conversation. We want your priority to be reconnecting with your child and
			transitioning to the next phase of the day.
		</p>
		<p>
			That said, we are also very excited to see you and have time with parents around! We hope to
			have time to update you on your child's day, share about experience or answer a question. Thank
			you for remembering that we are still at work actively supervising children, and can only
			listen with partial attention as we may need to walk away from a conversation to support the
			children or check in with another parent. This will likely not be a good time for sensitive
			conversations or administrative type questions, but we can always schedule a time for these
			types of conversations to happen.
		</p>
		<p>
			While you are on campus you will likely gain a new perspective of the friendships and play that
			happen at our school. You are welcome to play with your child or just watch them at their work.
			If you ever feel that there is a conflict, or other situation with another child that concerns
			you or might need the support of an adult, we ask that you alert a teacher right away and let
			us be the one to handle the situation. You are always welcome to step in with your own child,
			but we do ask parents to refrain from warning, redirecting, scolding, helping, or intervening
			with other children in our care.
		</p>
		<p>
			Please do not take photos or videos while you are here, many children have specific photo
			release plans and are trusting us to protect their child's privacy.
		</p>

		<h2>The FIVE Things to Bring From Home</h2>
		<p>
			<strong>Wet bag</strong>, full of <strong>extra clothes</strong>. Blue bag, full of
			<strong>gear</strong>. Rest bag, full of <strong>bedding</strong>. Water bottle.
		</p>

		<h3>Extra clothes</h3>
		<p>
			Please bring at least two <em>full</em> change of seasonally appropriate clothes, including 4 pairs
			of socks. These items will live inside the school bathroom in a bin labeled with your child's name,
			which will be in the bathroom on the floor where they sleep. Their items need to fit comfortably
			inside the bin. Hair needs to be pulled back and out of your child's face so consider tossing a
			few extra elastics/clips in their white bin!
		</p>

		<h3>Wet bag</h3>
		<p>
			We will provide your child with a wet bag that has two pockets: one for clean clothes and one
			for soiled clothes. When your child has soiled clothes that need to go home to be washed, they
			will be in the wet bag, on your child's hook in the covered area. Seeing a wet bag there is your
			signal to bring it home, wash those clothes and bring the wet bag back the next day with a
			clean set of replacement clothes. You will need to put the clean clothes and wet bag into your
			child's white bin inside the school, please do not leave it on their hooks, or in their blue
			bags or nap bags. It's important your child has their wet bag at school each day. If it becomes
			lost, or your family just needs a few extras, you can purchase more. We do ask that any "home
			wet bags" are the same style and labeled the same way as the school bags so your child can have
			consistency. If your child does not have a wet bag at school, you will get one "reminder
			plastic bag" and then after that there will be a $10 fee for each day your child doesn't have a
			wet bag at school. If your child needs a wet bag frequently, we recommend putting a week's
			worth in their bin at the start of the week.
		</p>

		<h3>Outdoor gear for the weather</h3>
		<p>
			These items live in your child's big blue bag on their hook in the covered area. All gear needs
			to be able to fit comfortably inside their blue bag. More information on gear can be found
			later in the handbook, but here is a quick glance:
		</p>
		<div class="overflow-x-auto">
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th>Season</th>
						<th>Gear</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr>
						<td>Fall</td>
						<td>Layers, waterproof jacket and rain pants or rain suit, boots</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Winter</td>
						<td>
							Warm waterproof coat, warm waterproof boots, hat/hood, gloves/mittens, rain
							pants/snow pants
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Spring</td>
						<td>Layers, waterproof jacket and rain pants or rain suit, boots</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Summer</td>
						<td>Sandals or sneakers, SPF shirts/hats</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td>Covered area</td>
						<td>A warm fleece or hoodie they can wear during meals instead of their rain gear</td>
					</tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<h3>Rest items</h3>
		<p>
			At minimum this will include a crib sheet and blanket. You can also send a small pillow and/or
			ONE stuffy if needed. If your child uses a pacifier please let us know and we will provide a
			labeled case for it. These items will live inside a provided "nap bag" that we will store in a
			closet during the week and send home over the weekend for you to wash and return. Any items
			soiled during the week we will wash on site. Children who occasionally wake up wet will be lent
			a wet pad which will be sent home weekly for you to wash and send back to school along with
			their bedding. All of your child's rest items must fit comfortably inside the provided nap bag
			so we can store them in a sanitary way. At school, it will progressively become your child's
			responsibility to take out their bedding, set up their cot, remove bedding from their cot and
			return their rest items to their nap bag. If you notice that YOU have to fold things just right
			to get it all in there, the odds are your child is going to really struggle with it at school,
			and you may need to trade out some things.
		</p>

		<h3>Water bottle</h3>
		<p>
			This will live in a crate/on a shelf in the covered area. Please make sure your child can open
			and close their water bottle independently and that the water bottle won't leak if tipped over.
			Please write your child's name on their water bottle and lid. We will run water bottles through
			the dishwasher weekly or as needed. They will be sent home during Fall, Winter, Spring and
			Summer break so you can disassemble and deep clean them at home.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Please write your child's name or initials on all items. Lost items? Check the lost and
			found tub.</strong>
		</p>

		<h3>Things to leave at home</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>All personal toys, games, books, technology gadgets</li>
			<li>Jewelry, costumes, make up</li>
			<li>
				Tie shoes, or complicated buckles. Please send them to school in shoes they can manage on
				their own, or replace the traditional laces with the curly "pull laces".
			</li>
			<li>Scarves, belts</li>
			<li>All food, drinks, and snacks</li>
			<li>
				Topical medications (sunscreen, chapstick, diaper cream, lotion, etc) need to be labeled
				with your child's name and given to a teacher. Due to safety concerns, they cannot be kept
				in your child's bag/pocket.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Outdoor Policy and Gear/Clothes Info</h2>
		<p>
			Chances are, you enrolled your child at this school in part because of our extended outdoor
			time. In order to be faithful to this, we are outside most of the day, in all kinds of weather,
			year round. Having the right gear and being able to put it on and take it off is a must. Some
			kids "run warm," some kids "run cold", and pretty much all of them run around the yard all day
			which keeps their blood pumping! The most important things to consider when choosing gear is
			keeping them DRY and giving them layering options. Talk with them about their different layers
			and when it may be a good time to take off the long sleeved shirt, pull on those mittens, or
			switch from rain boots to sneakers. Look at your weather app before leaving the house, look at
			the sky, and teach your child how to prepare well for their day. Bring their clothes and gear
			home when the seasons change and remove anything that is too small or not a good choice for the
			upcoming weather. Restock it with "yes" options for the current season and check the lost and
			found for things that belong to your child. It is your responsibility to make sure your child
			has the appropriate gear to safely and comfortably work and play outdoors for several hours.
			Out of respect to all the other children and families who come prepared, we are not able to
			stay inside because one family forgot their gear, and we may ask you to provide it before your
			child can stay and participate.
		</p>

		<h3>Clothes</h3>
		<p>
			Anticipate that your child may get dirty, painty, markered, glued, or otherwise messy while at
			school. We choose washable art supplies and encourage children to be responsible with their
			play, but sending them to school in clothes they should "try to keep clean" is probably not
			going to end well and will likely hinder their work. Hair should be pulled back out of faces and
			their clothes should be:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>Comfortable</li>
			<li>Easy to crawl, run, dance, and climb in</li>
			<li>
				Easy to put on and take off independently: for those over 18 months please avoid snaps,
				buttons, buckles and tight cuffs
			</li>
			<li>Clearly labeled with their name or initials</li>
		</ul>

		<h3>Shoes & Socks</h3>
		<p>
			While at school, your child will run, climb and walk on a variety of surfaces. It's a good idea
			to keep one extra pair of shoes at school. Most kids wear sneakers or enclosed sandals
			throughout the summer/fall/spring. We want children to be as independent as possible with shoes
			so avoid tie laces and buckles. They will also need boots for the fall/winter/spring; plastic
			rain boots and snow boots can work, but insulated rain boots seem to be a great multi-season
			option. Three to five pairs of "back up" socks should be plenty, wool-blend socks stay much
			drier and warmer than cotton. When the weather is warm, we allow children to choose to wear
			their shoes or take them off.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Suggestions:</strong> for 6mth-18 months, it's so tricky to find shoes that stay on
			little feet but also allow for movement. Boggs toddler boots are our favorite (soft shell for
			the youngest, hardshell for the steady walkers). Booties for young ones who aren't walking are
			important for dry days to keep dust/dirt/pokes off their fat little feet (harts stay on well).
			For 18 months and up, Keen sandals, Natives, or most styles of velcro shoes are great. Crocs
			can work as long as your child can run and climb comfortably in them. Bogg boots, or most
			things from Jan and Jul, REI, Columbia works great.
		</p>

		<h3>Outerwear</h3>
		<p>
			Layers are super important for children to learn to manage their temperature. Make sure to keep
			some type of medium/light outer layer at school. Examples are a hoodie sweatshirt, a flannel, a
			cardigan, a fleece, etc. If your child runs cold, a pair of sweats or leg warmers are nice in
			the cooler months and on chilly summer mornings. If they sunburn easily, a UV protective rash
			guard and sun hat are great for the hot days. Fleece onesies are great for Sprouts in the
			winter, especially for children who "run cold".
		</p>

		<h3>Rain Gear</h3>
		<p>
			Lots of outdoor gear is water <em>resistant</em>, which will not hold up for a full day in the
			rain. Double check that everything is labeled waterproof, weatherproof, or rainproof. You can
			either choose a one piece rainsuit, or pair pants with a coat. It's a good idea to have some
			back up rain gear if it's going to pour during the day. It can be miserable to put a damp cold
			jacket back on after rest time.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Rainsuits:</strong> some families really like the rain suits since it covers most of
			their clothes and it's just one thing to keep track of, plus they can basically go swimming and
			only their feet will get wet. This seems like the best option for the really little folks (under
			2.5) since they typically are still falling down often and are still fairly reliant on a teacher
			to help gear them up and make gear choices for them. We've found the suits to be a bit awkward
			for the older children to put on and zip up themselves and tend to make kids too warm in the
			spring/fall and make it tricky to wear a coat in the winter. That said, a few of our preschoolers
			do have rain suits that work very well for them.
		</p>
		<p>
			<strong>Two piece rain gear:</strong> on the bottom they'll need a good pair of rain pants which
			will not only protect them from rain, but also dewy slides, damp grass, muddy knees, and chilly
			air. If they run cold, snow pants or puffer pants are a great choice in the colder months. On the
			top a thin waterproof jacket that can be worn over a warm thick hoodie/sweater will easily allow
			them to adjust their layers. Fleece and wool sweaters are a great choice for colder months. This
			combo keeps them dry but not too warm year round. Those that run warm can opt for just their thin
			waterproof shell, but have the sweater handy if they get chilly. If they run cold or when the
			temperature is really low, then a warm waterproof winter jacket is a better choice. Double check
			the labels as many winter coats are not waterproof, and a wet puffer is heavy and cold. For
			Sprouts, the one piece suits are by far the easiest way to keep your child dry and their clothes
			kind of clean, especially if they are crawling/toddling with lots of tips and plops still.
			However, once they are actively toilet learning, we need to switch to a 2 piece set because
			there is just not enough time to take everything off!
		</p>

		<h3>Head/Hands</h3>
		<p>
			When it's warm and sunny it may be a good idea to send your child with a UV protection hat,
			especially if they burn easily. When it's cold, a wool/fleece hat or headband-style-ear-warmer
			is ideal. For their hands, mittens are way easier to put on and actually keep their hands
			warmer. Gloves can be tricky for the younger kids to put on by themselves, but do tend to be
			worn longer once because of the increased dexterity. Either way, it's important to teach them to
			put their mittens/gloves in their pockets when not being worn, or invest in some glove clips.
			For Sprouts, feel free to bring hats, we will absolutely try to keep them on your child. For
			hands, there just isn't a great solution. On the "too cold to be without" days, we highly
			recommend mittens (with or without thumb holes) that have a velcro strip at the wrist which we
			can tighten.
		</p>

		<h3>Where to buy</h3>
		<p>
			The Columbia brand tends to be a crowd favorite for light weight, reasonably priced gear that
			works great and lasts forever (buying it second/third hand is perfect!), they also have an
			outlet store in Woodburn and lots of sales online. Oakiwear and Jan and Jul make great kid stuff
			too, cuter color choices and patterns, sometimes wears out faster, but it'll easily last until
			they grow out of it. LL Bean, REI and other "outdoor/sport" stores often have reasonable options
			as well. Double check nothing groups, friends who have bigger kids, and things like craigslist/fb
			marketplace. Super Kids Resale is a great local option for gently used gear. Of course, there are
			always the options of old navy, target, and miscellaneous amazon brands, which will likely hold
			up for a season but maybe not any longer. It's also a great idea to trade/sell gear with other
			WPP families.
		</p>

		<h2>Tuition and Fees</h2>
		<p>
			Enrollment for the school year begins with a $500 deposit that holds your child's slot until
			September. At that point for new students, $300 will go towards their annual enrollment fee and
			$200 will be credited to September tuition. Returning families will only pay a $200 annual
			enrollment fee and so $300 will be credited to their September tuition.
		</p>
		<p>
			Tuition payment for the month is due on the 1st day of that month. Payments not made in full by
			5:30pm on the 5th of the month will accrue a $25 late fee. Returned checks will accrue a $25
			late fee. If the account is not paid in full by the 10th of the month the child may not return
			to care until the account is brought current. Accounts remaining unpaid beyond the 10th of the
			month may be grounds to terminate the contract.
		</p>
		<p>
			You can pay us using all forms of checks, money orders, or using Zelle (find us listed under
			our email address, if it pops us as "Wild Primrose" that's us too!). We do not accept cash.
			Checks need to have your child's name written on them somewhere and can be mailed or dropped
			into the black box just inside the screen door of 9433. All payments must be received during
			business hours. Payments made after business hours will not be processed until the next
			business day, which may cause payments to be late and accrue late fees.
		</p>
		<p>
			The first two weeks of care are a trial period, during which time either party may terminate
			without advanced notice. After the trial period, any modifications families would like to make
			to their contract, including termination will require a written notice thirty days in advance.
			Any family giving a 30 day notice will still be required to pay for that 30 days, regardless of
			attendance.
		</p>
		<p>
			Children are not allowed to be dropped off before 7:30am. Children are required to be picked up
			before 5:30pm. Late pick ups will acquire an additional $1 fee for every minute the child is
			left in care past 5:30pm. The time will be determined by our clock. Repetitive late pick ups
			may be grounds for termination.
		</p>
		<p>
			Find a friend! If you refer a family to our program and they decide to enroll their child, you
			will receive a $50 credit to your account during their second month of enrollment.
		</p>

		<h2>WP Trading Days Policy</h2>
		<p>
			We know that families cannot always attend on their scheduled days, and other times they may
			need childcare on a day they aren't currently scheduled for. In order to help families get the
			care they need we have partnered with one of our skilled preschooler parents, Daniel Ray, to
			develop the day trader app. Once you set up your account you can freely buy/sell/trade days with
			other families in our community. Here are a few tips to keep in mind as you navigate the
			process:
		</p>
		<ol>
			<li>
				When you buy/sell days using the day trader app, the transaction happens entirely between
				families and WPP isn't directly involved. We consider the day to be owned by the family who
				paid us tuition for it until either A) we get a notification from the app that says the day
				has been <em>paid for</em> by another family, or B) we get a text/email from the selling family
				telling us that another family bought/traded for a day. A child may only attend care if they
				currently own the day, so please take care not to bring your child on a day you have sold to
				another family.
			</li>
			<li>
				It is up to the "selling family" to clearly state what they are selling, the price/trade
				they are asking for, and how they want to be paid.
			</li>
			<li>
				It's up to the "buying family" to be sure they want what is being sold and to both indicate
				they want the day and make the payment directly to the selling family.
			</li>
			<li>
				Once the day is paid for, it's the responsibility of the selling family to either mark
				"verified payment" on the app and it will notify us, or if not using the app they need to
				send us and email/text that says "my child will not be there on this date, this family has
				their day!"
			</li>
			<li>
				Once we hear from the app/selling family, then we change it on our attendance and the buying
				family now owns the day and that child is who we'll accept into care that day.
			</li>
			<li>
				It is not likely that we will approve a Preschooler to buy the day from a Sprouts child or
				visa versa. However, you are more than welcome to try and we will definitely assess if it
				makes reasonable sense to make an exception.
			</li>
		</ol>

		<h2>Waitlist</h2>
		<p>
			Families wishing to enroll their child at Wild Pear Preschool can be placed on the waitlist if
			there isn't an immediate opening. In order to be waitlisted, families need to submit a
			completed application form and a non-refundable $20 fee. Being placed on the waitlist does not
			guarantee enrollment at a later date. It does, however, guarantee that the child will have
			priority to enroll in any open slots as they become available.
		</p>
		<p>
			When a slot becomes available, we will consider which family on the waitlist would be the best
			fit, with a goal to create well balanced cohorts of children while striving to coordinate each
			family's preferred schedule. For this reason, we do ask that parents give us as much
			information as possible on their waitlist form and notify us of any changes that come up.
		</p>
		<p>
			Once offered a slot, families will need to make a choice to either enroll or pass. If they
			choose to pass, they will remain on the waitlist and the slot will be offered to another
			family. In most cases families will have up to one week to decide if they want the available
			slot. If they do, they will need to pay the $500 non-refundable deposit which will hold their
			slot for up to 3 weeks. $300 of the deposit will cover the annual enrollment fee, while $200
			will be applied to the first month's tuition.
		</p>
		<p>
			Priority is given to currently enrolled families, as such, a current family looking to change
			their slot or enroll a sibling will be given first consideration and is not required to pay the
			$20 fee.
		</p>

		<h2 id="health-policy-and-flow-chart">Health Policy and Flow Chart</h2>
		<p>
			Wild Pear's health policy is required to be in compliance with the Oregon Health Department and
			the Early Learning Department. This means that we can care for mildly ill children, however,
